title: A Thought on “Coding”
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

I've talked to many designers who, at some point, say they want to learn to code. But, they don't, really. They're designers — trained to look, to make, to shape how things feel. If they wanted to be programmers, they'd have gone a different route.

What a designer actually needs isn't code. It's the ability to design digital things that are usable, understandable, and buildable by someone else.

Consider the architect. An architect doesn't lay brick. They plan how a building looks, feels, and holds together, then hand that plan to the people who build it. Design works the same way: understand what's possible on a screen or in a network, then design within those constraints, for builders who aren't you.

The hard part was never the code. It's the vocabulary, knowing what's actually possible in the digital sphere. Print design has a small, fixed set of methods, easy to hold in the head: a few binding options, a few printing methods. Digital has many, and most stay invisible until you know to look for them. Learn the vocabulary, and you can design things other people can build.

